From: Jacob Cook <jacobmcook1984@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Input: axp20x-pek - do not report the key press that woke the system

When the power key is the wakeup source, the press is delivered to
userspace after resume and systemd-logind suspends the system straight
back to sleep.

The AXP288 handles this by clearing the pending PEK bits from the noirq
resume callback, but that only works for a real suspend-to-RAM where the
IRQ thread cannot run until the devices are back. Under s2idle the nested
regmap-irq thread runs as soon as the PMIC interrupt fires, consumes the
status bits and calls this handler while the system is still officially
suspended, so anything done at resume time is too late.

Drop key events while pm_suspend_in_progress() says a system suspend is in
progress. The interrupt has already done its job as a wakeup source; the
matching release, if it lands after resume, is a lone key-up that nobody
acts on.

Seen on the Anbernic RG35XX H (H700 + AXP717): every power-key wake from
s2idle was followed by "Power key pressed short" / "Suspending..." from
systemd-logind in the same second.

---
--- a/drivers/input/misc/axp20x-pek.c
+++ b/drivers/input/misc/axp20x-pek.c
@@ -26,6 +26,7 @@
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
+#include <linux/suspend.h>
 
 #define AXP20X_PEK_STARTUP_MASK		(0xc0)
 #define AXP20X_PEK_SHUTDOWN_MASK	(0x03)
@@ -201,6 +202,17 @@
 	struct axp20x_pek *axp20x_pek = input_get_drvdata(idev);
 
 	/*
+	 * A press that wakes the system from sleep has done its job as a
+	 * wakeup source. Under s2idle the nested IRQ thread runs as soon as
+	 * the PMIC interrupt fires, long before the devices resume, so the
+	 * pending bits cannot be cleared from a resume callback the way the
+	 * AXP288 does it. Drop the event instead of reporting it to
+	 * userspace, which would otherwise suspend the system right away.
+	 */
+	if (pm_suspend_in_progress())
+		return IRQ_HANDLED;
+
+	/*
 	 * The power-button is connected to ground so a falling edge (dbf)
 	 * means it is pressed.
 	 */
